ate that endogenous  gene expression  now of course it's much more um  complicated in real life and in cellular  biology than you know playing with lego  um so what we can see here is that the  pro the micrornas are uh transcribed in  these multi-hairpin  uh primary microrna structures  which are then cleaved into these  pre-microrna structures exported out of  the nucleus cleaved again by dicer into  the mature micrornas that we can see  over here and then there one of the  strands is incorporated into the  rna-induced silencing complex and this  is an argonaut protein complex that uses  that mrna to identify the target mr  micron aid to identify the target mrna  and when they find it they can either  cause  translational repression through  imperfect complementarity and this means  that  the argonal complex binds and will not  let it pass through the ribosome and  won't let it actually translate into  protein  or there's perfect complementarity which  results in mrna cleavage and  removal  now why am i talking about these in  terms of biomarkers if they've got such  an important role within the cell  what we've actually found over the years  is that  these micrornas can actually be released  into circulation and this could be  either due to cell stress or cell death  and a reduction in the membrane  integrity  they can be released either as naked  micro rna molecules or they can be bound  to lipoproteins or argonal proteins as  we can see over here onto the right  but they can also be uh packaged up and  released in these uh extracellular  vesicles uh known as exosomes and this  is an active  process and can be used as cell to cell  communication  now what's really cool about these  particular molecules is that they show  remarkable stability within circulation  they are resistant to degradation um by  endogenous nucleases they can withstand  multiple freeze thorough cycles changes  in temperature changes in ph they're  quite stable and good in terms of you  know 