re is the results  of that  feature selection or that micron a  selection we can see that even just  starting with one microrna the top  microrna gave us actually above 90  percent accuracy but once we hit three  micrornas that's where we see our  optimal  and these are the three micrornas that  form that signature uh and we can see  that it's really close to 100 accurate  um precise and  uh sensitive there and kind of adding in  more and more micrornas really doesn't  improve uh the model very much at all so  going from 55 differentially expressed  micrornas down to three uh in a machine  learning model  what's really interesting about these  guys these particular micrornas is that  this they're not the most abundant one  is quite um high abundance so merge  four two three um  one is kind of middling and 195 is not  low but it's you know on the lower end  of the scale there uh what we also see  is they're not the most differentially  uh expressed either we have some that  are you know one that's down two that's  up but they're not you know sitting out  here right on the fringes  and when we actually have a look at the  micrornas only two of them  actually came up in the differential  expression analysis so mer-23a  looks like it's uh down-regulated but  it's non-significant  whereas the other two are up regulated  and so this it's an interesting  comparison between what's found  via  the machine learning versus what is in  the machine  learning model there as well  so when we actually have a look at how  well this um predictive signature works  uh after that sort of a thousand random  iterations we actually get about a 99.9  accuracy 99.8  precision and again 99.9 recall or  sensitivity uh using just those three  micrornas and when we actually plot this  uh using a pca we see a really nice  separation between those two groups and  what we can see here as well is this has  the probability of each of these samples  being classified as infected with covert  19. what we can see is that the healthy  is  basically zero percent probability  pervert 19 is 100  probability and that probability only  starts to kind of waver right near that  decision line which is this black line  that you can see uh dividing those two  groups so it was a highly confident  model  what was interesting is then when we  could look at the further visits um  visit two three and four of these  individuals uh as they started getting  better as they started clearing this  disease  most of the samples started  congregating and grouping back with the  healthy controls down here  now there is an obvious exception to  this rule um visit 2 3 and 4 denoted  here by the hash symbol  these were from one individual who was  in icu and was actually sick through all  visits and  their  samples clustered more with the infected  signature so this  kind of suggests that it's a signature  of really early disease that starts to  fade away over time as people start to  clear that disease  all right so we we found this um  particular signature in um you know a  smallish number of patients and we  wanted to then go okay can we validate  this using an animal model and we used  ferrets as the  preferred animal model  they're used quite significantly in  terms of respiratory viral infections  particularly influenza  so we used a ferret model where 20  ferrets  were uh intranasally inoculated with  sars cov2  and they were seriously sacrificed on  different days and on each one of these  days we also had  nasal swabs washes and bloods taken  now what we can see um so this is the  sars here v2 qpcr results we can see  that even from day three um most of them  show uh positivity particularly in the  turbine in the  nasal wash and oral swabs but it starts  to decrease over the days in fact by day  14 it's only detectable in the lymph  nodes and not anywhere else  so to really validate the human microrna  signature we investigated serum from  ferrets that were either uninfected  uh infected with sars here v2 or just to  kick up the diff uh difficulty a little  bit uh infected with influenza or h1n1  strain  what we can see is when we put together  a pca based on these three micrornas we  can already see that they're starting to  kind of cluster out pretty nicely we've  got our uninfected over here in blue  h1n1 down there in orange and the sarsia  v2 infected  ferrets there in green and you can see  that they are actually starting to  cluster out without doing anything to  them apart from looking at those three  micrornas  and when we actually just look at um  ferrets infected with sciatica v2 versus  those that are uninfected we get again  about over 99  accuracy and actually 100 recall and  rocket auc and we get this really nice  uh really confident model between um  these two groups it's really nice to see  kind of validating what we had seen in  the human cohort but again i guess one  of the questions we get asked a lot is  can this differentiate between multiple  different infections and that's why we  brought in the influenza the h1n1  infected  ferrets there as well  the good news is it can so  we had a look at again using these three  micrornas  to  assess whether we could uh differentiate  between uninfected sarzia v2 infected  and h1n1 infected ferrets and the  accuracy of this is still pretty high  it's about 95  accurate in determining uh or predicting  whether these samples belong to one of  these three groups  and what you can see is actually one of  the misclassifications that's done there  which would be pulling down this  accuracy score it's misclassifying one  of the h1n1 infected ferrets as sars  cov2 that you can see down there on the  white dot there or the light dot  all right so i'm just going to spend a  couple of minutes having a bit of a chat  about some of the newest data that we've  started to put together  so oftentimes what we get asked about is  what about  non-invasive nasal swabs um wouldn't  that be better than pla