  as well  all right so getting into the summary uh  what have we done and what's next uh so  circulating micrornas uh respond to  sarsia v2 infection we found a large  proportion of micrornas that did change  in response to 55 differentially  expressed  micrornas we found a signature of three  plasma micrornas that can independently  classify covet 19 with 99.9  accuracy  and this signature is actually robust in  an animal model there as well with about  99.7 percent accuracy uh looking between  uh covert 19 also cv2 infection uh and  uninfected ferrets and can actually  distinguish between uninfected sarcia v2  and seasonal influenza h1n1 infected  ferrets with about 95 accuracy and this  includes cases uh  later on in the disease progression  where nasal swab pcr results were  actually negative  and we found a different set of three  micron a signature  that uh classifies copic 19 with 100  accuracy using that sort of nasal swab  media  now what's next um we're looking into  biomarker validation in larger patient  cohorts a lot of these obviously smaller  sample numbers um australia did quite  well at least in the initial parts of  this pandemic  so  samples weren't super easy to come by  we're hoping to to pad those numbers out  and really get into that biomarker  validation and have a look at sort of  clinical sensitivity and specificity and  we really want to have a look at really  interested in looking in those pre and  asymptomatic patients to really see how  well  this signature can work in that cohort  as well  and we're also looking into commercial  partnerships for translation either as a  lab-based test or a point of care test  and of course a very big thank you to  all the people who helped out in this  particular study um i will definitely  acknowledge cam stuart or cameron  stewart down there  who's the team lead for host response  here at csiro um  chris khalid who helped with a lot of  the bioinformatics work um and actually  trained me to use pyth